{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Landim","name":"Landim, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungkyoung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jungkyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Mariani","name":"Mariani, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Gamma Expansion of the Level Two Large Deviation Rate Functional for Reversible Diffusion Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60F10, 60J45, 60J60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:31:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-18T12:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:19:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fix a smooth Morse function $U\\colon \\mathbb{R}^{d}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with finitely many critical points, and consider the solution of the stochastic differential equation \\begin{equation*}\n d\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(t)=-\\nabla U(\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(t))\\,dt \\,+\\,\\sqrt{2ε}\\, d\\boldsymbol{w}_{t}\\,, \\end{equation*} where $(\\boldsymbol{w}_{t})_{t\\ge0}$ represents a $d$-dimensional Brownian motion, and $ε\u0026gt;0$ a small parameter. Denote by $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})$ the space of probability measures on $\\mathbb{R}^d$, and by $\\mathcal{I}_ε \\colon \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})\\to[0,\\,\\infty]$ the Donsker--Varadhan level two large deviations rate functional. We express $\\mathcal{I}_ε$ as $\\mathcal{I}_ε= ε^{-1} \\mathcal{J}^{(-1)} + \\mathcal{J}^{(0)} + \\sum_{1\\le p\\le \\mathfrak{q}} (1/θ^{(p)}_ε) \\, \\mathcal{J}^{(p)}$, where $\\mathcal{J}^{(p)}\\colon \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\to [0,+\\infty]$ stand for rate functionals independent of $ε$ and $θ^{(p)}_ε$ for sequences such that $θ^{(1)}_ε\\to\\infty$, $θ^{(p)}_ε/ θ^{(p+1)}_ε\\to 0$ for $1\\le p\u0026lt; \\mathfrak{q}$. The speeds $θ^{(p)}_ε$ correspond to the time-scales at which the diffusion $\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(\\cdot)$ exhibits a metastable behavior, while the functional $\\mathcal{J}^{(p)}$ represents the level two large deviations rate functional of the finite-state, continuous-time Markov chains which describe the evolution of the diffusion $\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(\\cdot)$ among the wells in the time-scale $θ^{(p)}_ε$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"67 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arumugam","familyName":"Nallanathan","name":"Nallanathan, Arumugam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Channel Estimation for Rydberg Atomic Quantum Receivers: Unrolled Phase Retrieval from Holographic Snapshots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:23:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T03:37:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:37:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T12:41:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:58:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lsp.2026.3685170","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A model-driven deep learning framework is proposed for channel estimation in Rydberg atomic quantum receivers (RAQRs) based on the measurement of holographic snapshots. Specifically, we develop a Transformer-based unrolling architecture, termed URformer, to solve the non-linear biased phase retrieval problem, which is derived by unrolling a stabilized variant of the expectation-maximization Gerchberg-Saxton (EM-GS) algorithm. Each layer of the proposed URformer incorporates three trainable modules: 1) a learnable filter network that replaces the fixed Bessel kernel in the classic EM-GS algorithm; 2) a trainable gating mechanism that adaptively combines classic updates to ensure training stability; and 3) an efficient channel Transformer module that learns to correct residual errors by capturing non-local channel dependencies. Numerical results demonstrate that the proposed URformer significantly outperforms classic iterative algorithms and conventional black-box neural networks with less pilot overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:50Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12709","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12709","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12709","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John M.","familyName":"Carroll","name":"Carroll, John M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing the Power of AI in Qualitative Research: Role Assignment, Engagement, and User Perceptions of AI-Generated Follow-Up Questions in Semi-Structured Interviews"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T08:35:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:45:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semi-structured interviews highly rely on the quality of follow-up questions, yet interviewers' knowledge and skills may limit their depth and potentially affect outcomes. While many studies have shown the usefulness of large language models (LLMs) for qualitative analysis, their possibility in the data collection process remains underexplored. We adopt an AI-driven \"Wizard-of-Oz\" setup to investigate how real-time LLM support in generating follow-up questions shapes semi-structured interviews. Through a study with 17 participants, we examine the value of LLM-generated follow-up questions, the evolving division of roles, relationships, collaborative behaviors, and responsibilities between interviewers and AI. Our findings (1) provide empirical evidence of the strengths and limitations of AI-generated follow-up questions (AGQs); (2) introduce a Human-AI collaboration framework in this interview context; and (3) propose human-centered design guidelines for AI-assisted interviewing. We position LLMs as complements, not replacements, to human judgment, and highlight pathways for integrating AI into qualitative data collection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to IJHCS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12709","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:03:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allen","familyName":"Schmaltz","name":"Schmaltz, Allen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:19:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T06:08:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:27:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T19:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:04:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T17:12:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-17T01:04:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:40:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:23:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:41:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1109","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the Similarity-Distance-Magnitude (SDM) activation function, a more robust and interpretable formulation of the standard softmax activation function, adding Similarity (i.e., correctly predicted depth-matches into training) awareness and Distance-to-training-distribution awareness to the existing output Magnitude (i.e., decision-boundary) awareness, and enabling interpretability-by-exemplar via dense matching. We further introduce the SDM estimator, based on a data-driven partitioning of the class-wise empirical CDFs via the SDM activation, to control the class- and prediction-conditional accuracy among selective classifications. When used as the final-layer activation over pre-trained language models for selective classification, the SDM estimator is more robust to covariate shifts and out-of-distribution inputs than existing calibration methods using softmax activations, while remaining informative over in-distribution data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026. 22 pages, 8 tables, 2 algorithms. (v6 adds Appendix A.10 and Alg. 2.) arXiv admin note: substantial text overlap with arXiv:2502.20167"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:46:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaz","familyName":"Furniturewala","name":"Furniturewala, Shaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkaitz","familyName":"Zubiaga","name":"Zubiaga, Arkaitz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:51:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-23T09:33:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-27T23:01:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:57:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adversarial perturbations can reduce state-of-the-art toxicity classifiers to near-zero accuracy, yet existing defences treat models as black boxes. We apply mechanistic interpretability to toxicity classification for the first time, identifying the internal attention-head circuits responsible for both correct classification and adversarial vulnerability. Across a 2$\\times$2 factorial study (BERT $\\times$ RoBERTa) $\\times$ (Jigsaw $\\times$ ToxiGen), extended to Llama Guard~2 (8B), we show that zeroing a single attention head recovers up to 70.4 pp of adversarial accuracy for RoBERTa on Jigsaw and 37.3 pp for Llama Guard 2 on ToxiGen, at $\\leq$0.6 pp clean cost. Vulnerable heads generalise to held-out examples within $\\leq$1 pp, and a class-imbalance sweep confirms they act as selective toxic-class detectors. Head suppression matches or outperforms adversarial training on Jigsaw; data augmentation dominates on ToxiGen: a dataset-specific reversal explained by whether the classifier encodes a concentrated bottleneck or a distributed circuit. Demographic analysis across 20 Jigsaw and 13 ToxiGen minority groups reveals structurally unequal adversarial vulnerability, exposing mechanistically traceable fairness gaps in current toxicity classifiers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashika","familyName":"Ghai","name":"Ghai, Yashika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Del-Castillo-Negrete","name":"Del-Castillo-Negrete, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. A.","familyName":"Spong","name":"Spong, D. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. T.","familyName":"Beidler","name":"Beidler, M. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Runaway electron interactions with whistler waves in tokamak plasmas: energy-dependent transport scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:29:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T15:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T02:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Resonant interactions between high energy runaway electrons (REs) and whistler waves are a promising mechanism for RE mitigation in tokamak plasmas. While prior studies have largely relied on quasi-linear diffusion models in simplified geometries, we present a first-principles-informed framework that models RE-whistler interactions in a 3D tokamak equilibrium. This is achieved by coupling AORSA, which computes whistler eigenmodes for a given tokamak plasma equilibrium, and KORC, a kinetic orbit code that tracks full orbit RE trajectories in prescribed wave fields. Our results demonstrate that REs undergo scattering to large pitch angles and exhibit anomalous diffusion in both pitch-angle and kinetic energy space. Crucially, we observe a transition between diffusive, sub-diffusive, and super-diffusive transport regimes as a function of initial RE energy - an effect not captured by existing quasi-linear models. This anomalous transport behavior represents a significant advancement in understanding RE dynamics in the presence of wave - particle interactions. By identifying the conditions under which anomalous diffusion arises, this work lays the theoretical foundation for designing targeted, wave-based mitigation strategies in future tokamak experiments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:42:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12390","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12390","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12390","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Psomiadis","name":"Psomiadis, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Tsiotras","name":"Tsiotras, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Event-Triggered Distance-Based Formation Control for Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:32:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-03T20:55:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T00:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-16T20:10:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper addresses collaborative formation control for multi-agent systems with limited resources. We consider a team of robots tasked with achieving a desired formation from an arbitrary initial configuration. To reduce unnecessary control updates and conserve resources, we propose a distributed event-triggered formation controller. Unlike the well-studied linear formation controllers, the proposed controller is nonlinear and relies on inter-agent distance measurements. Control updates are triggered only when the measurement error exceeds a state-dependent threshold, ensuring system stability while reducing actuation effort. We also employ a distributed control barrier function to guarantee inter-agent collision avoidance. The proposed controller is validated through simulations and real-world experiments involving different communication topologies, scalability tests, and variations in design parameters, and is also compared to periodic triggering strategies. Results demonstrate that the event-triggered approach significantly reduces control effort while preserving formation performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12390","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lydia B","familyName":"Chilton","name":"Chilton, Lydia B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DoubleAgents: Human-Agent Alignment in a Socially Embedded Workflow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:43:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:19:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T16:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:57:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T16:19:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:37:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-14T18:33:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-16T00:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning agentic AI with user intent is critical for delegating complex, socially embedded tasks, yet user preferences are often implicit, evolving, and difficult to specify upfront. We present DoubleAgents, a system for human-agent alignment in coordination tasks, grounded in distributed cognition. DoubleAgents integrates three components: (1) a coordination agent that maintains state and proposes plans and actions, (2) a dashboard visualization that makes the agent's reasoning legible for user evaluation, and (3) a policy module that transforms user edits into reusable alignment artifacts, including coordination policies, email templates, and stop hooks, which improve system behavior over time. We evaluate DoubleAgents through a two-day in-lab interactive simulation study (n=10), three real-world deployments, and a technical evaluation. Participants' comfort in offloading tasks and reliance on DoubleAgents both increased over time, correlating with the three distributed cognition components. Participants still required control at points of uncertainty - edge-case flagging and context-dependent actions. We contribute a distributed cognition approach to human-agent alignment in socially embedded tasks. We further introduce interactive simulation as a methodological testbed for rapid iteration and alignment testing of agentic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures. ACM Conference on Human-AI Complementarity and Alignment (HCOMP 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:46Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12811","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12811","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12811","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binquan","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Binquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiting","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Feiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-23T10:53:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:54:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown remarkable prowess in text generation, yet producing long-form, factual documents grounded in extensive external knowledge bases remains a significant challenge. Existing \"top-down\" methods, which first generate a hypothesis or outline and then retrieve evidence, often suffer from a disconnect between the model's plan and the available knowledge, leading to content fragmentation and factual inaccuracies. To address these limitations, we propose a novel \"bottom-up,\" data-driven framework that inverts the conventional generation pipeline. Our approach is predicated on a \"Retrieval-First for Knowledge, Clustering for Structure\" strategy, which first establishes the \"knowledge boundaries\" of the source corpus before any generative planning occurs. Specifically, we perform exhaustive iterative retrieval from the knowledge base and then employ an unsupervised clustering algorithm to organize the retrieved documents into distinct \"knowledge clusters.\" These clusters form an objective, data-driven foundation that directly guides the subsequent generation of a hierarchical outline and the final document content. This bottom-up process ensures that the generated text is strictly constrained by and fully traceable to the source material, proactively adapting to the finite scope of the knowledge base and fundamentally mitigating the risk of hallucination. Experimental results on both 14B and 32B parameter models demonstrate that our method achieves performance comparable to or exceeding state-of-the-art baselines, and is expected to demonstrate unique advantages in knowledge-constrained scenarios that demand high fidelity and structural coherence. Our work presents an effective paradigm for generating reliable, structured, long-form documents, paving the way for more robust LLM applications in high-stakes, knowledge-intensive domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12811","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si-Yuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Si-Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokai","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangkai","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zhangkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Ming","name":"Ming, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui-liang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Hui-liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrent Cross-View Object Geo-Localization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T10:40:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:53:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-view object geo-localization (CVOGL) aims to determine the location of a specific object in high-resolution satellite imagery given a query image with a point prompt. Existing approaches treat CVOGL as a one-shot detection process, directly regressing object locations from cross-view information aggregation, but they are vulnerable to feature noise and lack mechanisms for error correction. In this paper, we propose ReCOT, a Recurrent Cross-view Object geo-localization Transformer, which models CVOGL as a recurrent localization process. ReCOT introduces a set of learnable tokens that encode task-specific intent from the query image and prompt embeddings, and iteratively attend to the reference features to refine the predicted location. To enhance this recurrent process, we incorporate two complementary modules: (1) a SAM-based knowledge distillation strategy that transfers segmentation priors from the Segment Anything Model (SAM) to provide clearer semantic guidance without additional inference cost, and (2) a Reference Feature Enhancement Module (RFEM) that introduces hierarchical attention to emphasize object-relevant regions in the reference features. Extensive experiments on CVOGL benchmarks demonstrate that ReCOT achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance while significantly reducing parameters compared to previous SOTA approaches. Our code is available at https://github.com/Temperature-ai/ReCOT.git."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13211","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13211","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13211","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Testa","name":"Testa, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Quarantiello","name":"Quarantiello, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric Nuertey","familyName":"Coleman","name":"Coleman, Eric Nuertey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samrat","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Samrat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julio","familyName":"Hurtado","name":"Hurtado, Julio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Lomonaco","name":"Lomonaco, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAM: Efficient Continual Learning at Scale via Grouped LoRA Adapter Merging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:18:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:55:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T15:02:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T13:56:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:42:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:38:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ability to learn continuously over time remains a major challenge for modern machine learning systems, even in the era of Foundation Models. While the rich representations learned by large pre-trained models can partially mitigate catastrophic forgetting, they still struggle to adapt efficiently to evolving data distributions. A key challenge remains, how to continually add new knowledge to a large pretrained model in a way that is scalable and computationally efficient over long task sequences. In this work, we introduce GLAM, a simple and effective framework for class-incremental continual learning based on LoRA adapters merging. For each task, GLAM trains a lightweight low-rank adapter with an importance scalar, incurring minimal computational overhead. Adapters are then pruned, rescaled, and sequentially grouped to enable structured knowledge reuse and limit parameter growth. At inference, all groups are combined into a single module, ensuring constant computational cost regardless of the number of tasks. We evaluate GLAM on vision benchmarks with sequences of up to 50 tasks, significantly extending beyond standard protocols. GLAM achieves the highest accuracy across the evaluated benchmarks. Compared with the baseline attaining the highest average accuracy across benchmarks, it uses 16--19\\% of the trainable parameters and reduces training time by approximately 63--74\\%, demonstrating efficient and scalable continual learning. Our source code is publicly available at https://github.com/atlas-luiss/GLAM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the Conference on Lifelong Learning Agents (CoLLAs 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13211","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge Acuña","familyName":"Flores","name":"Flores, Jorge Acuña","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"De Nittis","name":"De Nittis, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier Lorca","familyName":"Espiro","name":"Espiro, Javier Lorca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Higher Abelian Quantum Double Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 81R15, Secondary:46L30, 47L40, 82B20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:19:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:56:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops a rigorous $C^*$-algebraic framework for higher abelian quantum double models, generalizing Kitaev's construction to simplicial complexes of arbitrary finite dimension and local regularity. We fully characterize the frustration-free ground state space through an associated algebra of logical operators: its state space is shown to be homeomorphic to the space of frustration-free ground states, and to obey generalized canonical commutation relations. When the relevant homology and cohomology groups are finite, the logical algebra decomposes into a commutative factor and a full matrix algebra, thereby separating the classical and quantum parts of the frustration-free ground state structure. We further prove that the vanishing of these groups is necessary and sufficient for the core algebra of the model to form a Cartan pair with the full algebra of observables, a property expected to pave the way toward classifying the model's equilibrium (KMS) states, extending recent results for the planar case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages. v2: Added a new section (Section 4) on the (C^*)-diagonal structure and corrected minor typos. Keywords:Quantum Double Models, Frustration Free Ground States, Pure States Characterization, $C^*$-algebra, Cartan pair"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:22Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13196","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13196","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13196","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjian","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doruk","familyName":"Tuncel","name":"Tuncel, Doruk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Koerner","name":"Koerner, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Runkler","name":"Runkler, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Few-shot Dilemma: Over-prompting Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T20:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:03:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fllm67465.2025.11391015","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over-prompting, a phenomenon where excessive examples in prompts lead to diminished performance in Large Language Models (LLMs), challenges the conventional wisdom about in-context few-shot learning. To investigate this few-shot dilemma, we outline a prompting framework that leverages three standard few-shot selection methods - random sampling, semantic embedding, and TF-IDF vectors - and evaluate these methods across multiple LLMs, including GPT-4o, GPT-3.5-turbo, DeepSeek-V3, Gemma-3, LLaMA-3.1, LLaMA-3.2, and Mistral. Our experimental results reveal that incorporating excessive domain-specific examples into prompts can paradoxically degrade performance in certain LLMs, which contradicts the prior empirical conclusion that more relevant few-shot examples universally benefit LLMs. Given the trend of LLM-assisted software engineering and requirement analysis, we experiment with two real-world software requirement classification datasets. By gradually increasing the number of TF-IDF-selected and stratified few-shot examples, we identify their optimal quantity for each LLM. This combined approach achieves superior performance with fewer examples, avoiding the over-prompting problem, thus surpassing the state-of-the-art by 1% in classifying functional and non-functional requirements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted for the main track of IEEE FLLM conference; see the DOI and report number below"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13196","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:47:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Samples of Stationary Distributions of Weakly Interacting Diffusion Models Without Finite Particle Truncation: A Weak Generative Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:01:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T15:33:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:45:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computing the stationary probability density and generating corresponding samples for the mean-field model of an infinite number of weakly interacting diffusion particles pose significant numerical challenges, particularly in the phase transition regime where the interchangeability of infinite-time and infinite-particle limits breaks down. Traditional approaches, such as direct simulation of finite-particle systems, often fail to accurately pinpoint multiple stationary distributions in the mean-field meta-stable setting. On the other hand, solving the high-dimensional McKean-Vlasov partial differential equation using neural networks typically yields only the density function, limiting its utility for estimating statistical quantities from generating samples. In this work, we propose a novel generative framework based on the weak PDE formulation of the mean-field model to address these challenges. Our approach simultaneously computes the stationary distributions of McKean-Vlasov processes and generates independent and identically distributed samples that satisfy these distributions. This integrated approach not only reveals the true stationary distributions without the random perturbation of finite particle truncation, but also offers deeper insight into the system's behavior in the mean-field limit. Extensive numerical experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method, showcasing its ability to accurately approximate stationary distributions, capture intricate phase transitions, and handle high-dimensional complex systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:51:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingli","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Lingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanshan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shanshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Danyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Chenxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Grey Literature Review of AI-Native Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:01:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:51:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T08:34:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:35:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has given rise to AI-native applications, a new paradigm in software engineering that fundamentally redefines how software is designed, developed, and evolved. Despite their growing prominence, AI-native applications still lack a unified engineering definition and architectural blueprint, leaving practitioners without systematic guidance for system design, quality assurance, and technology selection.\n Objective: This study seeks to establish a comprehensive understanding of AI-native applications by identifying their defining characteristics, key quality attributes, and typical technology stacks, as well as by clarifying the opportunities and challenges they present.\n Method: We conducted a grey literature review, integrating conceptual perspectives retrieved from targeted Google and Bing searches with practical insights derived from leading open-source projects on GitHub. A structured protocol encompassing source selection, quality assessment, and thematic analysis was applied to synthesize findings across heterogeneous sources.\n Results: We finally identified 106 studies based on the selection criteria. The analysis reveals that AI-native applications are distinguished by two core pillars: the central role of AI as the system's intelligence paradigm and their inherently probabilistic, non-deterministic nature. Critical quality attributes include reliability, usability, performance efficiency, and AI-specific observability. In addition, a typical technology stack has begun to emerge, comprising LLM orchestration frameworks, vector databases, and AI-native observability platforms. These systems emphasize response quality, cost-effectiveness, and outcome predictability, setting them apart from conventional software systems.\n Conclusion: This study is the first to propose a dual-layered engineering blueprint..."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-22T02:18:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruimeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ruimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Jihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haosheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Haosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite-Agent Stochastic Differential Games on Large Graphs: II. Graph-Based Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T22:11:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:10:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T16:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-22T01:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a novel neural network architecture, called Non-Trainable Modification (NTM), for computing Nash equilibria in stochastic differential games (SDGs) on graphs. These games model a broad class of graph-structured multi-agent systems arising in finance, robotics, energy, and social dynamics, where agents interact locally under uncertainty. The NTM architecture imposes a graph-guided sparsification on feedforward neural networks, embedding fixed, non-trainable components aligned with the underlying graph topology. This design enhances interpretability and stability, while significantly reducing the number of trainable parameters in large-scale, sparse settings. We theoretically establish a universal approximation property for NTM in static games on graphs and numerically validate its expressivity and robustness through supervised learning tasks. Building on this foundation, we incorporate NTM into two state-of-the-art game solvers, Direct Parameterization and Deep BSDE (backward stochastic differential equation), yielding their sparse variants (NTM-DP and NTM-DBSDE). Numerical experiments on three SDGs across various graph structures demonstrate that NTM-based methods achieve performance comparable to their fully trainable counterparts, while offering improved computational efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:28Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-22T02:18:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cenlin","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Cenlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianlei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jianlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rubing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingkun","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Lingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weisheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Weisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HPIM: Heterogeneous Processing-In-Memory-based Accelerator for Large Language Models Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:04:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:42:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T06:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:29:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T09:21:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T00:34:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of large language models (LLMs) presents significant challenges due to their enormous memory footprints, low arithmetic intensity, and stringent latency requirements, particularly during the autoregressive decoding stage. Traditional compute-centric accelerators, such as GPUs, suffer from severe resource underutilization and memory bandwidth bottlenecks in these memory-bound workloads. To overcome these fundamental limitations, we propose HPIM, the first memory-centric heterogeneous Processing-In-Memory (PIM) accelerator that integrates SRAM-PIM and HBM-PIM subsystems designed specifically for LLM inference. HPIM employs a software-hardware co-design approach that combines a specialized compiler framework with a heterogeneous hardware architecture. It intelligently partitions workloads based on their characteristics: latency-critical attention operations are mapped to the SRAM-PIM subsystem to exploit its ultra-low latency and high computational flexibility, while weight-intensive GEMV computations are assigned to the HBM-PIM subsystem to leverage its high internal bandwidth and large storage capacity. Furthermore, HPIM introduces a tightly coupled pipeline strategy across SRAM-PIM and HBM-PIM subsystems to maximize intra-token parallelism, thereby significantly mitigating the serial dependency of the autoregressive decoding stage. Comprehensive evaluations using a cycle-accurate simulator demonstrate that HPIM significantly outperforms state-of-the-art accelerators, achieving a peak speedup of up to 23.1x compared to the NVIDIA A100 GPU. Moreover, HPIM exhibits superior performance over contemporary PIM-based accelerators, highlighting its potential as a highly practical and scalable solution for accelerating large-scale LLM inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:21Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianguang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qianguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongli","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Irampa","name":"Irampa, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few to Big: Prototype Expansion Network via Diffusion Learner for Point Cloud Few-shot Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:29:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:36:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T12:18:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:54:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Few-shot 3D point cloud semantic segmentation aims to segment novel categories using a minimal number of annotated support samples. However, prototypes derived from the limited non-structural point cloud support set are often misaligned and have a small capacity, hindering effective gen eralization to novel categories. This stems from two core issues: i) the prototype possess limited representational capacity fails to cover the full intra-class diversity of a novel category, and ii) the prototypes suffer from misalignment with the query space due to the inter-set inconsistency between support and query sets. To address these issues, our work focuses on leveraging the few support samples to construct a well-aligned big-capacity prototype. Motivated by the powerful generative capabilities of diffusion models, we re-purpose its pre-trained conditional encoder to provide rich feature components for prototype ex pansion. Subsequently, a push-pull force aligns this expanded prototype towards the query feature space. Under this setup, we introduce the Prototype Expansion Network (PENet), a framework that constructs aligned big-capacity prototypes from two complementary feature sources. Specifically, PENet employs a dual-stream learner architecture: it retains a conventional fully supervised Intrinsic Learner (IL) to distill representative features, while introducing a novel Diffusion Learner (DL) to provide rich generalizable features. The resulting dual prototypes are then processed by a Prototype Assimilation Module (PAM), which adopts a push-pull attention block to align the prototypes with the query space. Furthermore, a Prototype Calibration Mechanism (PCM) regularizes the final big-capacity prototype to prevent semantic drift. Extensive experiments on the S3DIS and ScanNet datasets demonstrate that PENet outperforms state-of-the-art methods across various few-shot settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:41Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-01T02:27:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingle","familyName":"Que","name":"Que, Xingle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiachi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangshun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guangshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Large Language Models Meet UAV Projects: An Empirical Study from Developers' Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:14:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T11:30:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:38:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T08:46:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:50:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) have become increasingly popular in our daily lives and have attracted significant research interest in software engineering. At the same time, large language models (LLMs) have made notable advancements in language understanding, reasoning, and generation, making LLM applications in UAVs a promising research direction. However, existing studies have largely remained in preliminary exploration with a limited understanding of real-world practice, which causes an academia-industry gap and hinders the application of LLMs in UAVs. To address this, we conducted the first empirical study to investigate how LLMs support UAVs. To characterize common tasks and application scenarios of real-world UAV-LLM practices, we conducted a large-scale empirical study involving 997 research papers and 1,509 GitHub projects. The results classified nine common tasks (e.g., Natural Language Command Parsing) in four UAV workflows (e.g., Information Input) undertaken by LLMs in real-world UAV projects and revealed a large difference in the task distribution of research efforts and industry practices. To gain deeper insight into these differences and understand developers' perspectives on the application of LLMs in UAVs, we conducted a survey of practitioners, receiving 52 valid responses from 15 countries. The results revealed that while 40.4% of developers have attempted to apply LLMs to UAV tasks, 59.6% still face challenges integrating their UAV projects with advanced LLM capabilities. Their feedback attributes these challenges to five factors, including technological maturity, performance, safety, cost, and others, and provides practical implications for researchers and developers in conducting UAV-LLM practices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautam","familyName":"Sreekumar","name":"Sreekumar, Gautam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu Naresh","familyName":"Boddeti","name":"Boddeti, Vishnu Naresh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InPhyRe Discovers: Large Multimodal Models Struggle in Inductive Physical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:07:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T04:52:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:45:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-31T19:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:22:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large multimodal models (LMMs) encode physical laws observed during training, such as momentum conservation, as parametric knowledge. It allows LMMs to answer physical reasoning queries, such as the outcome of a potential collision event from visual input. However, since parametric knowledge includes only the physical laws seen during training, it is insufficient for reasoning in inference scenarios that follow physical laws unseen during training. In such novel physical environments, humans could adapt their physical reasoning based on provided demonstrations. This inductive physical reasoning ability is indispensable for LMMs if they are to replace human agents in safety-critical applications. Despite its importance, existing visual benchmarks do not evaluate inductive physical reasoning and only consider the parametric knowledge in LMMs. To this end, we propose InPhyRe, the first visual question answering benchmark to measure inductive physical reasoning in LMMs. InPhyRe evaluates LMMs' ability to predict the outcome of collision events in algorithmically generated synthetic videos. By inspecting over 13 open-source and proprietary LMMs, InPhyRe informs us that (1) LMMs struggle to apply their limited parametric knowledge about universal physical laws to reasoning, (2) inductive physical reasoning in LMMs is weak when the physical laws underlying inference scenarios were unseen during training, and (3) inductive physical reasoning in LMMs suffers from language bias and may ignore the visual inputs, questioning the trustworthiness of LMMs regarding visual inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to TMLR. 53 pages including appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:27Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangmin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Qianyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangying","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiangying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxue","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiaxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixian","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Lixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:08:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T07:14:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T17:23:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:16:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deploying Large Language Models (LLMs) in medical applications requires fact-checking capabilities to ensure patient safety and regulatory compliance. We introduce MedFact, a challenging Chinese medical fact-checking benchmark with 2,116 expert-annotated instances from diverse real-world texts, spanning 13 specialties, 8 error types, 4 writing styles, and 5 difficulty levels. Construction uses a hybrid AI-human framework where iterative expert feedback refines AI-driven, multi-criteria filtering to ensure high quality and difficulty. We evaluate 20 leading LLMs on veracity classification and error localization, and results show models often determine if text contains errors but struggle to localize them precisely, with top performers falling short of human performance. Our analysis reveals the \"over-criticism\" phenomenon, a tendency for models to misidentify correct information as erroneous, which can be exacerbated by advanced reasoning techniques such as multi-agent collaboration and inference-time scaling. MedFact highlights the challenges of deploying medical LLMs and provides resources to develop factually reliable medical AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to The Fifth Workshop on Generation, Evaluation, and Metrics (GEM) at ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:28Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengrui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hengrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ScaleDoc: Scaling LLM-based Predicates over Large Document Collections"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:18:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:18:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T06:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:31:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3802106","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicates are foundational components in data analysis systems. However, modern workloads increasingly involve unstructured documents, which demands semantic understanding, beyond traditional value-based predicates. Given enormous documents and ad-hoc queries, while Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate powerful zero-shot capabilities, their high inference cost leads to unacceptable overhead. Therefore, we introduce \\textsc{ScaleDoc}, a novel system that addresses this by decoupling predicate execution into an offline representation phase and an optimized online filtering phase. In the offline phase, \\textsc{ScaleDoc} leverages a LLM to generate semantic representations for each document. Online, for each query, it trains a lightweight proxy model on these representations to filter the majority of documents, forwarding only the ambiguous cases to the LLM for final decision. Furthermore, \\textsc{ScaleDoc} proposes two core innovations to achieve significant efficiency: (1) a contrastive-learning-based framework that trains the proxy model to generate reliable predicating decision scores; (2) an adaptive cascade mechanism that determines the effective filtering policy while meeting specific accuracy targets. Our evaluations across three datasets demonstrate that \\textsc{ScaleDoc} achieves over a 2$\\times$ end-to-end speedup and reduces expensive LLM invocations by up to 85\\%, making large-scale semantic analysis practical and efficient."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:24Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:29:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Baharoon","name":"Baharoon, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siavash","familyName":"Raissi","name":"Raissi, Siavash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John S.","familyName":"Jun","name":"Jun, John S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thibault","familyName":"Heintz","name":"Heintz, Thibault","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Alabbad","name":"Alabbad, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Alburkani","name":"Alburkani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sung Eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent","familyName":"Kleinschmidt","name":"Kleinschmidt, Kent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulrahman O.","familyName":"Alhumaydhi","name":"Alhumaydhi, Abdulrahman O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohannad Mohammed G.","familyName":"Alghamdi","name":"Alghamdi, Mohannad Mohammed G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy Francis","familyName":"Palacio","name":"Palacio, Jeremy Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Bukhaytan","name":"Bukhaytan, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah Michael","familyName":"Prudlo","name":"Prudlo, Noah Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rithvik","familyName":"Akula","name":"Akula, Rithvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brady","familyName":"Chrisler","name":"Chrisler, Brady","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Galligos","name":"Galligos, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed O.","familyName":"Almutairi","name":"Almutairi, Mohammed O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mazeen Mohammed","familyName":"Alanazi","name":"Alanazi, Mazeen Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nasser M.","familyName":"Alrashdi","name":"Alrashdi, Nasser M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel Jihwan","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Joel Jihwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sri Sai Dinesh","familyName":"Jaliparthi","name":"Jaliparthi, Sri Sai Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke David","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Luke David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathaniel","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Nathaniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathvik","familyName":"Suryadevara","name":"Suryadevara, Sathvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed F.","familyName":"Mohammed","name":"Mohammed, Mohammed F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yevgeniy R.","familyName":"Semenov","name":"Semenov, Yevgeniy R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun-Hsing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kun-Hsing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulrhman","familyName":"Aljouie","name":"Aljouie, Abdulrhman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"AlOmaish","name":"AlOmaish, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Rodman","name":"Rodman, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Rajpurkar","name":"Rajpurkar, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RadGame: An AI-Powered Platform for Radiology Education"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:27:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-17T00:26:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T00:55:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce RadGame, an AI-powered gamified platform for radiology education that targets two core skills: localizing findings and generating reports. Traditional radiology training is based on passive exposure to cases or active practice with real-time input from supervising radiologists, limiting opportunities for immediate and scalable feedback. RadGame addresses this gap by combining gamification with large-scale public datasets and automated, AI-driven feedback that provides clear, structured guidance to human learners. In RadGame Localize, players draw bounding boxes around abnormalities, which are automatically compared to radiologist-drawn annotations from public datasets, and visual explanations are generated by vision-language models for user missed findings. In RadGame Report, players compose findings given a chest X-ray, patient age and indication, and receive structured AI feedback based on radiology report generation metrics, highlighting errors and omissions compared to a radiologist's written ground truth report from public datasets, producing a final performance and style score. In a prospective evaluation, participants using RadGame achieved a 68% improvement in localization accuracy compared to 17% with traditional passive methods and a 31% improvement in report-writing accuracy compared to 4% with traditional methods after seeing the same cases. RadGame highlights the potential of AI-driven gamification to deliver scalable, feedback-rich radiology training and reimagines the application of medical AI resources in education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ML4H Version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:47:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13316","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13316","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13316","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Millicent","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Millicent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Mario Ceballos","familyName":"Arroyo","name":"Arroyo, Alberto Mario Ceballos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giordano","familyName":"Rogers","name":"Rogers, Giordano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naomi","familyName":"Saphra","name":"Saphra, Naomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byron C.","familyName":"Wallace","name":"Wallace, Byron C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Activation Verbalization Methods Convey Privileged Information?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:59:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T01:00:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T16:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:17:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T18:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-10T02:04:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T17:55:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-14T01:25:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent interpretability methods have proposed to translate LLM internal representations into natural language descriptions using a second verbalizer LLM. This is intended to illuminate how the target model represents and operates on inputs. But do such activation verbalization approaches actually provide privileged knowledge about the internal workings of the target model, or do they merely convey information about the inputs provided to it? We critically evaluate popular verbalization methods and datasets used in prior work and find that one can perform well on such benchmarks without access to target model internals, suggesting that these datasets are not ideal for evaluating verbalization methods. We then run controlled experiments which reveal that verbalizations often reflect the parametric knowledge of the verbalizer LLM that generated them, rather than the knowledge of the target LLM whose activations are decoded. Taken together, our results indicate a need for targeted benchmarks and experimental controls to rigorously assess whether verbalization methods provide meaningful insights into the operations of LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026. 41 pages, 23 tables, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13316","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:01:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:49:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erblin","familyName":"Isaku","name":"Isaku, Erblin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Sartaj","name":"Sartaj, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaukat","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Shaukat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beatriz","familyName":"Sanguino","name":"Sanguino, Beatriz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongtong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tongtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Houxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Peyrucain","name":"Peyrucain, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Out of Distribution Detection in Self-adaptive Robots with AI-powered Digital Twins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T11:43:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:54:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ase63991.2025.00281","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-adaptive robots (SARs) in complex, uncertain environments must proactively detect and address abnormal behaviors, including out-of-distribution (OOD) cases. To this end, digital twins offer a valuable solution for OOD detection. Thus, we present a digital twin-based approach for OOD detection (ODiSAR) in SARs. ODiSAR uses a Transformer-based digital twin to forecast SAR states and employs reconstruction error and Monte Carlo dropout for uncertainty quantification. By combining reconstruction error with predictive variance, the digital twin effectively detects OOD behaviors, even in previously unseen conditions. The digital twin also includes an explainability layer that links potential OOD to specific SAR states, offering insights for self-adaptation. We evaluated ODiSAR by creating digital twins of two industrial robots: one navigating an office environment, and another performing maritime ship navigation. In both cases, ODiSAR forecasts SAR behaviors (i.e., robot trajectories and vessel motion) and proactively detects OOD events. Our results showed that ODiSAR achieved high detection performance -- up to 98\\% AUROC, 96\\% TNR@TPR95, and 95\\% F1-score -- while providing interpretable insights to support self-adaptation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:05Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:02:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D%20AND%20%28%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20agent%20OR%20agents%20OR%20diffusion%20OR%20multimodal%20OR%20GPU%20OR%20MoE%20OR%20%22state%20space%22%20OR%20%22world%20model%22%20OR%20%22world%20models%22%20OR%20%22vision%20language%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}